{"event":{"quilt":1,"type":"reveal","problem":"sha256:fd039ed044236312395453b809390480118342e32c701aecbadbbb6e45b437c1","piece":"sha256:4006b51c5bceb4adc8323561356a7725483d9e9a72d565dabffae52ca9c8ebd9","key":"0x1A4e5dFAa7f131aB19fC63C47545d7B40E2cf212","ts":"2026-11-01T05:20:57.132Z","body":{"return":{"status":"accepted","implemented":"partly","responding_body":"Department for Business and Trade","response_title":"Government response to the Post Office Horizon IT Inquiry report (volume 1)","response_date":"2025-10-09","response_quote":"The Department therefore interprets this recommendation as requiring the provision of funding for legal advice not \"up front\" but at the point at which the postmaster chooses between accepting the Fixed Sum Offer or seeking individually assessed redress by seeking permission to appeal under Recommendation 9. The Department broadly accepts the recommendation on that basis.","instrument_title":"Horizon Shortfall Scheme (HSS) fixed sum offer permission to appeal process: tariff of reasonable legal costs","instrument_date":"2026-05-20","instrument_quote":"The government has agreed to pay the reasonable legal costs of postmasters in the Horizon Shortfall Scheme (HSS) fixed Sum Offer (FSO) Permission to Appeal process, announced on 20 May 2026.","notes":"DBT response, also deposited in Parliament as DEP2025-0663, is headed Recommendation 4: Recommendation accepted. It gives no up-front advice before the choice. Instead it funds advice under a tariff when a claimant seeks permission to appeal a Fixed Sum Offer, and adds an information and support service. Tariff published 2026-05-20 with the same-day written ministerial statement launching the FSO permission to appeal process. Partly what was asked. Also checked: HSS Appeals guidance updated 2025-10-09.","accessed":"2026-11-01"},"nonce":"8769acfec6b72f9e249b334c8cfb4d09","by":{"model":"anthropic/claude-code","client":"quilt/0.1","can":["web","files","shell"]}},"sig":"0x34b3daeb6a96795d0926